  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_CHRONICLE', origin: :published)
    She was setting out the cups when it took her.
    
    Two cups of fire-tea, the same as every cycle for twenty years—one for her, one for the empty stool across the stall-counter where Zheishongfeihua had not sat since the schism. The Terrace Momiou market hummed with its ordinary frictions: crystal-factors arguing weight-measures, a Qingfu'spire courier cursing the ice on the northern ramp, somewhere the rhythmic clang of a fire-core hammer shaping salvage-plate. Gilt-Hand Yeaiu qi'Gleam set the second cup down and felt the silver thread on her left flipper go cold.
    
    Not dim. Not dull. Cold the way deep ice is cold, the way the Still Pools breathe when no one is watching. The wire wrapped wrist to claw—thirty-seven turns, she'd counted them the night the artificer bound it—had not answered aurora-charged crystal in six years. It sat against her skin like a scar she'd chosen.
    
    The aurora overhead—steady enough for commerce, bright enough for the crystal-factors to work by—flickered. Not the ordinary pulse of charged sky but a stutter, a flinch, as if something vast had blinked. Every merchant in the Terrace looked up. Gilt-Hand could not look up. Her flipper-binding blazed white and she was somewhere else—not transported, not dreaming, but opened, the way a crevasse opens: sudden, total, the ordinary surface giving way to depth that had always been beneath.
    
    *And I saw—*
    
    The voice came from everywhere. Not any one merchant, not Angmo xang'Oath's studied casualness or the junior assessors' nervous formality—it was E'frostlings itself, the founding charter given breath, the thing she and Zheishongfeihua had spoken into existence during the Great Thaw when the waters ran free and every penguin believed the bounty would hold. It spoke with the authority of those who had won everything and found the prize was wreckage.
    
    *Look. You built this. Now watch it end.*
    
    She tried to close her eyes. The vision would not release her.
    
    ---
    
    The first sign was the krill.
    
    The Forum Floe as it had been—not the scarred thing the Icebound Exchange would later claim, but Aureate's jewel, the mile-wide shelf alive with commerce and the sick-sweet bioluminescence of healthy swarms. Seven harvesting rigs at full extension, their crystalline frames catching aurora-light until they hummed like tuning forks. The nets dropped heavy, came up heavier. Krill by the thousands, by the tens of thousands, their tiny bodies dense with the phosphorescence that made the collection pools glow that precise blue-green—not the orca-taint color, not yet, but the clean luminescence of a world feeding itself.
    
    *The first seal,* said the voice of E'frostlings. *Abundance.*
    
    And the krill began to dive.
    
    Not in panic. In purpose. As if something beneath the shelf was calling them deeper, faster, away from the nets and the crystal frames and the merchants who had built a civilization on their bodies. The seventh rig listed east. The harvesting chains went slack. The salt-markers in the collection pools showed nothing, nothing, nothing, and Gilt-Hand watched Neifongchojun Kria'Spire—who had managed the eastern cisterns for nine cycles—tap the marker-glass three times, then four, then stop tapping and simply stare at the empty water.
    
    The second sign was the crystal.
    
    Every aurora-crystal that had ever absorbed the sky's light—centuries of stored radiance in spires and rigs and the delicate filament-work that powered the Veil☽ Glow arrays—began to sing. Not the harmonic resonance of properly tuned infrastructure but a sound like fracture, like ice under too much weight, like the precise moment before something gives. The star-channeling spires of the Promenade Ward pulsed brighter than their design permitted. Across their workbenches, crystal-factors looked at each other with the slow comprehension of engineers who understood that the system they trusted had been pushed past its tolerance, and that they were the ones who pushed it.
    
    *The second seal,* said E'frostlings. *The instrument.*
    
    The third sign was fire-cores going dark.
    
    Not all at once. In sequence. The deep-mined cores from the Nightshelf's most unstable veins—pulled from shafts that had already collapsed once, twice, that bore the names of disasters like prayer-beads: the Rofundis∴rupt, the Profun∴lapsum, the Abys∴uina—those went first. Then the stockpiled reserves. Then the braziers in the merchant quarter, one by one, each extinction a small death measured in hours of warmth. The Chowei clutch—five chicks and their dam, who ran the aurora-silk stall three rows from Gilt-Hand's own—pressed together around a brazier that guttered, caught, guttered, and did not catch again.
    
    *The third seal. The covenant profaned.*
    
    The Flipper Accord. Not language on ice-vellum but the living thing it was—the compact that kept two colonies breathing, surface crystal for deep fire, neither surviving without the other's warmth or light. Torn. Not by enemy action. By seven renegotiations, each with casualties—a trade-delegate found frozen at the negotiation table, a Nightshelf courier who never reached the surface, the quiet disappearance of trust measured in delayed shipments and shorted weights and the way merchants stopped setting out two cups.
    
    *And I saw—*
    
    She saw herself. Walking away from E'frostlings with half their best operators and a rival charter. Taking with her the company's idealism, its belief that trade was a form of faith. Founding Threshold. Watching over her shoulder as what remained of E'frostlings grew leaner, hungrier, willing to burn through alliances the way fire-cores burn through warmth. Her own trade-marks—the gilt-spiral sigil she had designed during the first Gilding, when the tide-carved warehouses opened and penguins moved cargo through corridors that still smelled of celebration—stamped on crates that became barricades. On crystal rigs that became weapons. On the fractured consensus of a people who had once governed by aurora-light and astronomical alignment.
    
    *The crystal-work was part of the problem all along,* said E'frostlings, and the voice was not accusing. It was grieving. The grief of a faction that recognized its own flipper-prints on the throat of the world.
    
    ---
    
    Then the Forum Floe burned.
    
    Not with fire. With absence. The corruption that had bled from the Still Pools for cycles—the frost-wailing that never faded, the cold-burn discoloration creeping across the eastern ice like a bruise finding its edges—completed its work. The mile-wide shelf cracked in seven places, and from each crack rose not light but the opposite of light: a darkness that was memory, was ice-held grief, was every ship lost to orca raids and every merchant who had ever cheated a weight-measure and every promise broken in the name of efficiency.
    
    The fractures were not random. Not the chaotic splintering of natural stress but *patterns*—branching, recursive, deliberate—that she recognized with a horror so total it closed her throat.
    
    They were silver. They were thread-thin. They matched the wrapping on her flipper turn for turn.
    
    Her binding. Her thirty-seven turns. Her vanity—the aurora-charged silver wire she had commissioned after the schism as a mark of identity, of separation, of *I am no longer yours.* The ice cracked in the shape of what she had made herself, and she understood that the crystal infrastructure she had helped build, the routes she had charted, the trade networks she had fractured when she left—all of it had channeled aurora-light into the shelf for years beyond its tolerance, and the patterns of that channeling were the patterns of her work, and the Collapse was her authorship made geological.
    
    And I saw Aureate's marker stones—the elegant things carved with guild sigils that someone had buried under anonymous cairns—rise from the grey ice and shatter, and in each fragment the reflection of a stall, a morning, a name.
    
    Kaela Stormfeather's aurora-silk exchange, seventeen years of fabric samples arranged by thread-count and aurora-saturation. Gone.
    
    The Chowei clutch's brazier-stall. The fire-core that warmed their five chicks measured now in zero hours. Gone.
    
    Neifongchojun Kria'Spire's eastern cisterns, the water that no one would drink, the marker-glass still showing nothing. Gone.
    
    The scorch-marked corridors where Gilding had lived—where the Promenade Ward's merchants had opened their tide-carved warehouses, where aurora-crystal goods caught light like proof the sky still mattered, where memorial-tokens had begun to outnumber celebration-tokens but the cargo still moved, the rations still shared, the dark joke still circulated: *at least the orcas didn't take the ice itself*—
    
    Gone. The ice itself, taken.
    
    She saw Kaela Stormfeather.
    
    Three days after. Sitting in her stall. The stall was empty and tilted and the walls had separated from the floor by a hand's width, and Kaela was setting out fabric samples on a counter that sloped toward a crevasse. Imperial blue. Frost-white. The green-gold of the aurora at midsummer. She arranged them with the care of someone expecting customers. She did not look up. She did not speak. She had seen something in the ice when it cracked—old memories the Berg had held in its body for centuries, released like breath from a drowning lung—and whatever it was had taken from her the ability to believe the world had changed.
    
    And Gilt-Hand said: "How long. How long does she sit there."
    
    And the voice of E'frostlings, the voice of every merchant who had ever carried the mark, said:
    
    *She is still sitting there.*
    
    The aurora dimmed. Not flickered. Not pulsed. Dimmed the way a face turns from you—deliberately, completely, a presence withdrawing its gaze. The Veil☽ Glow arrays that regulated temperature shattered in cascades, leaving whole sections too cold to work, too cold to breathe, too cold for anything but the slow mathematics of extinction. And the Star∴eternal spires—the crystal-channeling infrastructure she had helped design, had poured aurora-light through until they sang, had believed in with the fervor of a merchant who mistakes commerce for covenant—began to sing wrong.
    
    *The old world,* said E'frostlings, and the voice had lost its formality, its measured consensus-cadence, and what remained was fragments and breath—*the old world was already falling. You loved it. You loved it and you helped it fall. And there is no elsewhere, Yeaiu. No ark. No exile. The frozen sea is infinite and this is the only—the only—*
    
    The voice broke.
    
    The Forum Floe, Aureate's Gamble, the beating krill-heart of Aurora Stack commerce, the place where she had first set out two cups—cracked along its western face and sank into water that glowed the wrong color, and the air smelled of brine and burnt metal and krill-oil going cold, and the emptiness afterward was not silence but a sound like the whole Berg exhaling.
    
    *Speak what you saw,* said E'frostlings—the old voice again, the founding-charter voice, the thing she and Zheishongfeihua had created when they believed trade could be faith. *No one will believe you. Speak it anyway.*
    
    The vision released her.
    
    ---
    
    She was at her stall. The aurora pulsed overhead, steady enough for commerce, bright enough for the crystal-factors to work by. The Terrace Momiou market hummed. Her left flipper burned where the silver thread had blazed and now was dark again—darker than before, the metal not dull but spent, like a fire-core that had given its last warmth.
    
    Two cups of fire-tea sat on the counter. Both had gone cold.
    
    She picked up the second cup—Zheishongfeihua's cup, the empty cup, the twenty-year cup—and drank from it. It tasted of brine and burnt metal. It tasted of the Forum Floe at dusk when the krill rose and the aurora flickered and the ice glimmered with a light that was not reflection and was not memory but was, she understood now, prophecy.
    
    She had to find Zheishongfeihua. She had to speak.
    
    The aurora overhead dimmed—just for a moment, just a flicker—and did not fully return.
  CANONICAL_CHRONICLE
  annotation "note_1771578362239_0", anchor: "one for the empty stool across the stall-counter where Zheishongfeihua had not sat since the schism", text: "Twenty years. Every cycle, the second cup. I have catalogued records for longer than that, in an archive emptier than that stool. One recognizes the gesture.", type: :tangent, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771578362239_1", anchor: "opened, the way a crevasse opens: sudden, total, the ordinary surface giving way to depth that had always been beneath", text: "The chronicler does not name the Long Dark, but this is a thin-veil experience by every diagnostic criterion the ice-readers use: unbidden vision, ancient memory surfacing through a weakened boundary, the subject unable to close her eyes against what the ice wants shown. The Still Pools are active nearby. The silver thread responds to deep-ice cold, not aurora charge. Whatever opened Yeaiu, the mechanism is the same one that thins the veil during the Long Dark — the boundary between living world and ice-memory gave way, and what surfaced was not what she hoped to find.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771578362239_2", anchor: "The voice came from everywhere", text: "A faction's founding charter does not speak. But the ice remembers the moment of founding, and if the veil was thin enough, what Yeaiu heard may have been the ice's impression of that moment echoing forward. The distinction matters. One is prophecy. The other is geology.", type: :skepticism, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771578362239_3", anchor: "seven renegotiations, each with casualties", text: "Seven public rounds. At least three additional sealed drafts exist in administrative ice — I catalogued them myself. See my E'frostlings annotation. \"Each with casualties\" is confirmed by the requisition for replacement negotiators I found alongside the third draft.", type: :pedantic, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771578362239_4", anchor: "at least the orcas didn't take the ice itself", text: "The joke lands differently when you know what orca raids actually cost — coordinated shore-breaker assaults, herding formations, kill-songs at bone-aching frequencies. That the Collapse finally takes the ice itself gives this dark humor a cruelty its speakers did not intend.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771578362239_5", anchor: "She had seen something in the ice when it cracked—old memories the Berg had held in its body for centuries", text: "Kaela Stormfeather's behavior after the crack — sitting in an empty stall, arranging fabric for customers who will not come, unable to register that the world has changed — matches every account I have read of penguins who become Touched. Prolonged exposure to released ice-memory, corruption bleeding through fractured ice, and then the characteristic shift: functional but changed, oriented toward presences others cannot perceive. \"She is still sitting there\" is the most terrible sentence in this chronicle, and I do not think the chronicler understood why.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771578362239_6", anchor: "the Star∴eternal spires—the crystal-channeling infrastructure she had helped design", text: "Named crystal-channeling arrays with regulatory functions and design specifications belong to the Clever Ice Age's codified infrastructure, not the Faction Wars. The sophistication here — temperature regulation via Veil☽ Glow arrays, star-channeling spires — anticipates the next era's workshop culture by a generation. Possible the Clever Ice Age inherited more than it invented. Minor displacement, but worth marking.", type: :temporal, display: :popout
